var weeklyVsBiweeklyBody = '<h1>Weekly vs Every-Other-Week Dog Poop Service in Chattanooga: Which One Do You Actually Need?</h1>' +
  '<p>Scoopy Doo weekly dog poop removal in Chattanooga is $20 per visit for the first dog and every-other-week service is $33 per visit for the first dog. Over a year that is $1,040 versus $858, a difference of about $15 a month for twice as many visits. Twice-weekly service is $18 per visit. All pricing is per visit with no contract.</p>' +
  '<h2>Is every-other-week dog poop service enough, or do I need weekly?</h2>' +
  '<p>Every-other-week service is enough for one small dog on a yard of a quarter acre or more, especially in winter. Weekly service is the better fit for two or more dogs, small fenced yards, homes where children play in the grass, and the humid Chattanooga stretch from May through September when waste breaks down slowly and draws flies. Most Scoopy Doo customers with more than one dog end up on weekly.</p>' +
  '<h2>What weekly, twice-weekly, and every-other-week service cost per year</h2>' +
  '<table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Per visit (1 dog)</th><th>Visits per year</th><th>Per year</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
  '<tr><td>Twice weekly</td><td>$18</td><td>104</td><td>$1,872</td><td>Three or more dogs, small yards, summer</td></tr>' +
  '<tr><td>Weekly</td><td>$20</td><td>52</td><td>$1,040</td><td>Most homes with one or two dogs</td></tr>' +
  '<tr><td>Every other week</td><td>$33</td><td>26</td><td>$858</td><td>One dog, larger yard, lighter use</td></tr>' +
  '</tbody></table>' +
  '<h2>Why does every-other-week cost more per visit?</h2>' +
  '<p>Two weeks of accumulated waste takes noticeably longer to find and clear than one week of waste, so the higher per-visit rate reflects a bigger job per stop. That is why the yearly gap between the two plans is only $182, far smaller than the per-visit gap suggests.</p>' +
  '<h2>Can I switch between weekly and every-other-week service?</h2>' +
  '<p>Yes. Scoopy Doo has no contracts, so you can move between weekly, twice-weekly, and every-other-week at any time and pause service while you travel. Many Chattanooga customers run weekly from April through October and drop to every other week for the cold months. There are no cancellation fees.</p>' +
  '<h2>What stays the same on every plan</h2>' +
  '<p>Whichever schedule you choose, the visit is identical: an on-the-way text before arrival, a full grid-pattern sweep of the entire yard, all waste double-bagged into your outdoor bin, or hauled off the property for $5 per visit, the gate latched, and a gate photo sent when the job is done. You do not need to be home. See full rates at scoopychatt.com/pricing or get a free quote at scoopychatt.com/quote.</p>';

var petWasteStationsBody = '<h1>Pet Waste Stations for Chattanooga Apartments and HOAs: Placement, Servicing, and What It Costs</h1>' +
  '<p>Scoopy Doo LLC installs, stocks, and empties pet waste stations for apartment communities, HOAs, condo associations, and dog parks across Chattanooga TN and North Georgia, including Ringgold, Rossville, Fort Oglethorpe, and Flintstone GA. Stations are serviced on a scheduled route so they never overflow, and the same visit includes a grid-pattern sweep of common areas. Scoopy Doo is fully insured and proof of insurance is available to boards and property managers on request.</p>' +
  '<h2>How many pet waste stations does an apartment community need?</h2>' +
  '<p>A practical rule is one station for every 40 to 50 pet-friendly units, placed so no resident walks more than about 150 feet off their normal dog-walking route to reach one. Communities with an on-site dog park usually need one at the entrance and one inside. The right number for a specific property comes out of a free walkthrough, because building layout and where residents actually walk their dogs matter more than unit count alone.</p>' +
  '<h2>Where should pet waste stations be placed?</h2>' +
  '<p>Put stations at each main pedestrian exit, at the entrance to and inside any dog park, along the grass strips residents actually use, and near mail kiosks and trash compactors. Avoid placing them outside ground-floor windows and patios, next to pool decks or grill areas, or anywhere a service vehicle cannot reach to empty them.</p>' +
  '<h2>What does scheduled pet waste station service include?</h2>' +
  '<p>Every visit restocks each dispenser with bags, empties and re-lines each receptacle, wipes down the units, and hauls all waste completely off the property rather than into your dumpster. The same trip includes a grid-pattern sweep of common areas. Service is typically weekly or twice weekly, and communities with a busy dog park usually need twice weekly through the humid Chattanooga summer.</p>' +
  '<h2>How much do pet waste stations cost for an HOA in Chattanooga?</h2>' +
  '<p>Pet waste stations are $299 per station installed and $10 per station per week to service. Installation covers the station, post, mounting, and initial stock of bags. Servicing covers weekly restocking, emptying, re-lining, and hauling the waste off site. A community with four stations is $40 a week, about $173 a month or $2,080 a year. The common-area sweep that runs alongside the stations is quoted per property after a free walkthrough, since it depends on how much ground is covered and how often.</p>' +
  '<h2>Do HOAs need a contract for pet waste removal?</h2>' +
  '<p>Not with Scoopy Doo. Most commercial and HOA clients use simple month-to-month scheduling with no long-term commitment, which lets a board trial the service for a season before committing budget. If your association requires a formal written agreement for its records, we will work with that. Request a free property walkthrough at scoopychatt.com/quote or call 423-600-5040.</p>';

var partyCleanupBody = '<h1>One-Time Yard Cleanup Before a Party in Chattanooga or North Georgia: When to Book and What It Costs</h1>' +
  '<p>Scoopy Doo books one-time yard cleanups seven days a week across Chattanooga TN and North Georgia. One-time cleanups start at $85, take a single visit, and require no contract and no ongoing service. Most new customers are on the schedule within two to five days, so for a Saturday event, request a quote by Tuesday or Wednesday.</p>' +
  '<h2>How far ahead of a party should I schedule dog poop cleanup?</h2>' +
  '<p>One to two days before the event is ideal. That is close enough that a dog cannot undo the work and far enough that the grass dries and settles and any lingering smell fades. Booking a week ahead is wasted money if you have dogs, and booking the morning of leaves no margin if it rains or the schedule slips.</p>' +
  '<h2>How much does a one-time yard cleanup cost before an event?</h2>' +
  '<p>One-time yard cleanups start at $85. The final price depends on how long it has been since the yard was cleaned, how large the area is, and how many dogs use it. A well-kept yard needing a reset before guests sits near the starting price; a yard untouched since spring costs more. You get the exact figure before the visit.</p>' +
  '<h2>What is included in a one-time cleanup visit</h2>' +
  '<p>You get an on-the-way text before the technician arrives, a full grid-pattern sweep of the entire yard including corners and fence lines, all waste double-bagged and hauled off the property at no extra charge, since haul-away is included on one-time cleanups, the gate latched, and a gate photo confirming it. You do not need to be home.</p>' +
  '<h2>Will a one-time cleanup get rid of the smell in my yard?</h2>' +
  '<p>Removing the waste removes the source, which handles most of it. In Chattanooga and North Georgia humidity a heavily used yard can still hold odor compounds in the grass and topsoil right after a cleanup. For most yards the fix is time plus water: clean one to two days ahead and water the heavily used areas well afterward. For yards with years of buildup, Scoopy Doo also offers deodorizing and sanitizing at $20 per visit, which neutralizes those compounds instead of masking them.</p>' +
  '<h2>Do you serve North Georgia for short-notice event cleanups?</h2>' +
  '<p>Yes. Ringgold, Rossville, Fort Oglethorpe, and Flintstone GA get the same seven-day availability and the same rates as the Tennessee service area, with no travel surcharge. Short-notice requests are worth asking about because the schedule shifts daily. Request a quote at scoopychatt.com/quote or call 423-600-5040.</p>';

var yardSmellBody = '<h1>How to Get Rid of Dog Poop Smell in a Chattanooga Yard (What Actually Works)</h1>' +
  '<h2>Why does my yard still smell like dog poop after I clean it up?</h2>' +
  '<p>Because the smell is no longer coming from the waste. Bacteria and the ammonia and sulfur compounds they produce soak into the thatch layer and the top inch or two of soil, and they stay there after the solids are gone. Chattanooga humidity keeps those compounds volatile so they keep lifting off the ground, and our clay-heavy soil drains slowly so what soaks in sits near the surface.</p>' +
  '<h2>What is the fastest way to get rid of dog poop smell in a yard?</h2>' +
  '<p>Remove every bit of waste including the flattened pieces along fence lines, get the bags off the property rather than leaving them in a bin, then water the affected areas heavily. A deep soak dilutes the odor compounds and carries them down past the root zone instead of leaving them at the surface. Give it two or three days. For a yard kept up on a normal schedule that is the entire fix, and it costs nothing but water.</p>' +
  '<h2>Does lime get rid of dog poop smell in a yard?</h2>' +
  '<p>Garden lime raises soil pH and can reduce ammonia odor slightly, but it is not an odor treatment, and overapplying it damages the lawn and is not something you want dogs walking through. Water is more effective, cheaper, and cannot hurt the grass. Scented sprays only mask the smell for a few hours, and bleach kills grass, is unsafe around pets, and runs off into storm drains.</p>' +
  '<h2>How much does yard deodorizing cost in Chattanooga?</h2>' +
  '<p>Scoopy Doo charges $20 per visit for deodorizing and sanitizing, and it can be added to any recurring plan or to a one-time cleanup. It neutralizes the odor compounds and bacteria in the soil rather than masking them with fragrance, and it is safe for pets, children, and lawn health. It is worth paying for when a yard has years of buildup, when one area takes nearly all the traffic, or when an event is coming up and there is no time to wait for watering to work. For a yard on a weekly or every-other-week schedule you usually do not need it.</p>' +
  '<h2>How do I keep the smell from coming back?</h2>' +
  '<p>Odor is a symptom of accumulation. A yard cleaned every week never builds up enough in the soil to develop a smell in the first place. Weekly service is $20 per visit for the first dog and every-other-week is $33 per visit, neither with a contract. If the yard is badly behind, start with a one-time cleanup from $85. Free quotes at scoopychatt.com/quote.</p>' +
  '<h2>What if the smell is sharp and ammonia-like instead of foul?</h2>' +
  '<p>That is urine, not waste, and scooping will not touch it. It is usually worst in one small patch with yellowed grass around it. The fix is the same first move: soak it heavily and often, because urine salts are what burn the grass and dilution is the only thing that moves them.</p>';

